# Copy to .env and adjust. npm start and npm run dev load .env when it exists,
# using Node's built-in --env-file-if-exists, so no dotenv package is needed.
# docker compose reads it too, and passes APP_SECRET, MFA_KEY, the email
# settings and the site settings (SITE_URL and the rest) to the app
# (compose.yaml sets the rest itself).
# Real environment variables take precedence over values in this file.

# Secret for signing login tokens. Required when NODE_ENV=production;
# otherwise a random one is made each run and sign-ins end on restart.
# APP_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# NODE_ENV=production

# MongoDB connection string. Local development needs no sign-in. Production
# (deploy/compose.prod.yaml) runs MongoDB with --auth and one user who may read
# and write the glazecalc database only; deploy.sh writes this line, with the
# password from SSM Parameter Store, into /opt/glazecalc/app.env:
#   MONGODB_URI=mongodb://glazecalc:PASSWORD@mongo:27017/glazecalc?authSource=glazecalc
# The password is letters and digits only (openssl rand -hex 32), so it needs no
# percent-encoding in the string. See deploy/README.md, "MongoDB sign-in".
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1/glazecalc_app_dev

# npm run db starts MongoDB on ./db with its cache capped at 1 GB (scripts/db.mjs).
# Set MONGOD when mongod is not on the PATH, for example:
#   MONGOD=D:\MongoDB\mongodb-win32-x86_64-windows-9.0.2\bin\mongod.exe
# MONGOD_DBPATH, MONGOD_PORT and MONGOD_CACHE_GB change the folder, port and cache.

# Port for npm start, which serves the app and its API (under /api) together.
# npm run dev ignores this: the dev server uses 3000 and the API 4000.
PORT=3000

# Email (password reset links and password-change notices). Outside production
# the default, log, prints each email to the console, which is enough to follow
# a reset link while developing. In production email is off until set up.
#   smtp  sends through SMTP_URL: Amazon SES in production, e.g.
#         smtps://USER:PASS@email-smtp.us-west-2.amazonaws.com:465, or Mailpit
#         (docker run -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit) at smtp://localhost:1025
#   file  writes each email as JSON into MAIL_DIR (used by the tests)
# MAIL_TRANSPORT=log
# SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
# MAIL_FROM=Glazecalc <no-reply@glazecalcapp.com>

# Where this copy of the site lives (server/lib/site.ts): links in emails, the
# sitemap, and the host passkeys belong to (WebAuthn's relying party is its host
# name, the expected origin the URL itself), so passkeys made on staging never
# appear for production. Never taken from the request. Required in production
# (deploy/compose.prod.yaml sets https://glazecalcapp.com); defaults to
# http://localhost:3000 elsewhere. APP_URL is its older name and still works.
# SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# A label for a copy that is not production: authenticator apps show
# "Glazecalc (staging)", and mail subjects and a banner on every page say so.
# Empty in production.
# SITE_LABEL=staging
# 1: search engines may not index this copy (X-Robots-Tag on every response,
# robots.txt disallows everything, no sitemap). Staging sets it.
# NOINDEX=1
# When set, mail goes only to these addresses or @domains (comma-separated);
# mail to anyone else is dropped and logged by error code only. Staging sets it
# to the owner's addresses, so its database can never email a real user.
# MAIL_ALLOWLIST=owner@example.com,@example.org

# The key that encrypts authenticator-app secrets and hashes recovery codes
# (server/lib/mfa_key.ts): 32 random bytes, base64 (openssl rand -base64 32).
# Required in production, one per environment, from SSM (/glazecalc/mfa-key;
# staging /glazecalc/staging/mfa-key); compose.yaml, which runs the app as
# production, falls back to a public test key; elsewhere a fixed development
# key is used, with a warning. Never change it once in use, and keep an offline copy: without it,
# authenticator apps and recovery codes stop working (passkeys do not need it).
# MFA_KEY=

# Rate limits on sign-in, sign-up, trials and password reset are per client
# address. RATE_LIMITS=off turns them off (the test suites do).
# RATE_LIMITS=off

# AI apps named by their address (CIMD) that ask for more than a public
# client, as ChatGPT's document does (server/oauth/cimd.ts cimdAuthMode;
# docs/adr/0024-authorization-server.md, "Amended: ChatGPT's document"). Off
# when unset: only public apps, as before.
#   public           such an app is taken as a public app if its document
#                    also lists none; nothing is fetched from its jwks_uri
#   private_key_jwt  as public, and an app whose document asks for
#                    private_key_jwt signs every token request with its key,
#                    fetched from its own site
# Try it on staging first (docs/integrations/directories.md, "2. ChatGPT").
# OAUTH_CIMD_AUTH=public

# Sign in with Google, GitHub and Microsoft (server/lib/oauth.ts): a provider
# is on, and its button shows, only when both of its settings are set. Each is
# registered with the callback SITE_URL + /api/auth/<provider>/callback
# (deploy/README.md, "Sign in with Google, GitHub and Microsoft").
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The server log hides quieter levels than this: debug, info (the default),
# warn, error or silent.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Trials ("Try it now"): accounts with a generated name and no email, removed
# 14 days after they start. GUEST_TRIAL=off turns them off. At most
# GUEST_MAX_ACTIVE run at once, and each keeps at most GUEST_MAX_RECORDS records
# of each kind (recipes, notes and so on).
# GUEST_TRIAL=off
# GUEST_MAX_ACTIVE=500
# GUEST_MAX_RECORDS=25
# An account keeps at most ACCOUNT_MAX_RECORDS records of every kind together.
# ACCOUNT_MAX_RECORDS=100000
